{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Diederich Krug (25 May 1821 – 7 April 1880) was a German pianist and composer.","title":"Diederich Krug"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was born in Hamburg and studied under Jakob Schmitt.","title":"Diederich Krug"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He wrote a large number of pieces for piano, around 350 in all, which were popular with amateur pianists.","title":"Diederich Krug"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Typical examples are gathered in the "Pianoforte-Album" which appeared as No. 1220 in the "Collection Litolff"; it contains eight works: "Chant d'Adieu" (Op. 307), "Le petit Chevalier" (Op. 312 No. 2), "Einsames Haideblümchen" (Op. 329, No. 2), "La petite Coquette" (Op. 306), "Minnelied" (Op. 289), "Tyrolienne" (Op. 308, No. 1), "Der Wachtelruf" (Op. 317 No. 2), and "Impromptu-Romance" (Op. 286).","title":"Diederich Krug"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Masques op. 34 is a work for piano written by Karol Szymanowski from 1915 to 1916.","title":"Masques (Szymanowski)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Hagith, Op.","title":"Hagith (opera)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" 25, is an opera in one act by the Polish composer and pianist Karol Szymanowski considered one of the greatest Polish composers of the 20th century.","title":"Hagith (opera)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The opera premiered at the Grand Theatre, Warsaw in 1922, nine years after its creation.","title":"Hagith (opera)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The libretto in German was written by the Viennese secessionist poet and Szymanowski's friend Felix Dörmann.","title":"Hagith (opera)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""A Maiden's Prayer" (original Polish title: "Modlitwa dziewicy " Op.","title":"Maiden's Prayer"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" 4, French: "La prière d'une vierge ") is a composition of Polish composer Tekla Bądarzewska-Baranowska (1834–1861), which was published in 1856 in Warsaw, and then as a supplement to the "" in 1859.","title":"Maiden's Prayer"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The piece is a medium difficulty short piano piece for intermediate pianists.","title":"Maiden's Prayer"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Some have liked it for its charming and romantic melody; others have described it as "sentimental salon tosh."","title":"Maiden's Prayer"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The pianist and academic Arthur Loesser described it as "this dowdy product of ineptitude."","title":"Maiden's Prayer"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Karol Maciej Szymanowski (] ; 6 October 188229 March 1937) was a Polish composer and pianist, the most celebrated Polish composer of the early 20th century.","title":"Karol Szymanowski"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He is considered a member of the late 19th-/early 20th-century modernist movement Young Poland and widely viewed as one of the greatest Polish composers.","title":"Karol Szymanowski"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Alexandre Tansman (12 June 1897 – 15 November 1986) was a Polish composer and virtuoso pianist of Jewish origin.","title":"Alexandre Tansman"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He spent his early years in his native Poland, but lived in France for most of his life, being granted French citizenship in 1938.","title":"Alexandre Tansman"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" His Polish identity influenced several orchestral and chamber works, such as "Rapsodie polonaise" and "Quatre Danses polonaises", and some guitar works, such as "Hommage à Lech Walesa" and "Hommage à Chopin".","title":"Alexandre Tansman"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" His music is often said to be primarily neoclassical, drawing on his Polish Jewish heritage as well as his French musical influences.","title":"Alexandre Tansman"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Karol Szymanowski
